  "lang": "de",
  "markdownContent": "# German 2: der, die, das — genders and plurals\n\nEvery German noun has a gender: der (masculine), die (feminine) or das (neuter). Learn each noun with its article and plural.\n\n## Words\n\n| Word | Plural | Meaning | Pronunciation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| der Mann | die Männer | man | /man/ |\n| das Kind | die Kinder | child | /kɪnt/ |\n| der Tisch | die Tische | table | /tɪʃ/ |\n| die Lampe | die Lampen | lamp | /ˈlampə/ |\n| das Buch | die Bücher | book | /buːx/ |\n| der Stuhl | die Stühle | chair | /ʃtuːl/ |\n| die Tür | die Türen | door | /tyːɐ̯/ |\n| das Fenster | die Fenster | window | /ˈfɛnstɐ/ |\n| der Schlüssel | die Schlüssel | key | /ˈʃlʏsl̩/ |\n| die Tasche | die Taschen | bag | /ˈtaʃə/ |\n| das Handy | die Handys | mobile phone | /ˈhɛndi/ |\n| die Leute | — | people | /ˈlɔʏ̯tə/ |\n\n## Phrases\n\n- **Das ist ein Buch.**: That is a book.\n- **Der Tisch ist groß.**: The table is big.\n- **Die Lampe ist neu.**: The lamp is new.\n- **Das Kind ist klein.**: The child is small.\n- **Die Bücher sind alt.**: The books are old.\n- **Wo ist mein Schlüssel?**: Where is my key?\n- **Ist das eine Tasche?**: Is that a bag?\n\n## Grammar: articles and plurals\n\n| | masculine | feminine | neuter | plural |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| the | **der** Tisch | **die** Lampe | **das** Buch | **die** Bücher |\n| a | **ein** Tisch | **eine** Lampe | **ein** Buch | — |\n\n- Always learn the article with the noun: *der Stuhl*, *die Tür*, *das Fenster*.\n- Helpful patterns: nouns in **-e** are often feminine (*die Lampe, die Tasche*); **-chen** and **-lein** are always neuter.\n- The plural article is always **die**. Plural endings vary: *-e (Tische), -er (Kinder), -en (Türen), -s (Handys)*, or none (*Fenster*), often with an umlaut (*Bücher, Stühle, Männer*).\n- *Leute* (people) exists only in the plural.\n",
